use std
into cloth.amni

Namespace cloth; imports math. Simulates cloth as particles joined by distance rules (position-based dynamics with small steps), with no dependency on the renderer: a triangle mesh goes in, and every step gives new positions and normals for the mesh's vertices. Several cloths live side by side in shared module arrays; a cloth is named by the int that `make` returns. All simulation numbers are `real`.

`Cloth` is a struct with these fields, in this order: where its particles start in the particle arrays and how many it has (`p0`, `np`), the same for its edges (`e0`, `ne`), its bends (`b0`, `nb`), its triangles (`t0`, `nt`) and its mesh vertices (`v0`, `nv`), all int; then the settings, all real with these defaults: `density` 0.2 (kilograms per square meter), `gravity` 9.81, `thickness` 0.005, `damping` 1.0, `air` 1.2, `drag` 1.28, `friction` 0.5, `stretch` 0.0 and `bend` 1.0 (how soft the two kinds of distance rules are, 0 is rigid); then `substeps` int, default 8; `limit` bool, default true (keep particles within reach of the pins); `stale` bool, default true (the reach limits must be rebuilt); `keep` bool, default false (the next step leaves `last` as it is, so that `last` stays the shape of an earlier step).

Module vars, all growable arrays that every cloth appends to: `cloths` (of Cloth); `pos`, `old`, `vel`, `nrm`, `last`, `rest`, `goal`, `from` and `push` (real, three numbers per particle: position now, position at the start of the substep, velocity, normal, position at the end of the step before, position when made, the point a pinned particle is held at, where a pinned particle was when the step began, and the wind's acceleration for this step); `inv` and `inv0` (real, one per particle: one over its mass as used now, which is 0 while pinned, and its value when free); `held` (bool per particle); `reach_to` (int per particle: the particle it must stay near, or -1) and `reach` (real per particle: how far it may be from it); `edges` (int, two particle numbers per edge) and `edge_len` (real per edge); `bends` (int, two particle numbers per bend) and `bend_len` (real per bend); `tris` (int, three particle numbers per triangle); `vmap` (int per mesh vertex: its particle). Particle numbers stored in these arrays count from the start of the shared arrays, not from the cloth's own first particle. Colliders, shared by all cloths: `balls` (real, four per sphere: center and radius), `pills` (real, seven per capsule: one end, the other end, radius), `floor_on` bool, default false, and `floor_y` real.

`make(p, stride int, at int, nv int, indices) int` (p: read-only f32 slice; indices: read-only u32 slice) builds a cloth from a mesh and returns its number. Vertex v's position is the three floats at `p[v * stride + at]`. `indices` holds three vertex numbers per triangle, each below nv.
- Particles: vertices whose three coordinates are exactly equal share one particle (meshes repeat vertices along texture seams). Find them with an open-addressing hash table over the coordinates' bit patterns so that 100,000 vertices stay fast. Particles are numbered in order of first appearance; `vmap` gets each vertex's particle. A new particle's `pos`, `old`, `last`, `rest`, `goal` and `from` are its position, its `vel` and `push` are zero, its `nrm` is (0, 1, 0), `held` false, `reach_to` -1, `reach` 0.
- Triangles: one per mesh triangle, as particle numbers; skip a triangle with two corners on the same particle.
- Mass: every kept triangle gives a third of its area times the default density, 0.2, to each of its three particles. `inv` and `inv0` are one over the mass, with the mass taken as at least 1e-9.
- Edges: every undirected pair of particles that is a side of a kept triangle, once (use a hash table keyed by the lower number times 4294967296 plus the higher), with the distance between the two as its length. Remember for each edge how many triangles use it and the corner opposite to it in the first two of them.
- Bends: for every edge used by exactly two triangles whose two opposite corners are different particles, the pair of those corners, with the distance between them as its length.
- Append the Cloth record with the defaults and return its index in `cloths`.

`pin(c int, vertex int, x real, y real, z real)` holds the particle of the cloth's mesh vertex `vertex` at the point: sets its `goal`, and when it was not held yet marks it held, sets its `inv` to 0 and marks the cloth stale. `unpin(c int, vertex int)` frees it again: when held, `held` becomes false, `inv` becomes `inv0` and the cloth is marked stale.

`reset()` forgets every cloth and every collider: it empties all the module arrays and turns the floor off.

`clear_colliders()` empties `balls` and `pills` and turns the floor off. `ball(x real, y real, z real, r real)` adds a sphere, `pill(ax real, ay real, az real, bx real, by real, bz real, r real)` adds a capsule, `ground(y real)` turns the floor on at that height.

`normals(c int)` recomputes the cloth's particle normals: zero them, add each triangle's cross product (the edge from its first to its second corner cross the edge from its first to its third, at the current positions) to its three particles, then scale each to length one, or set (0, 1, 0) where the sum is shorter than 1e-20.

`limits(c int)` rebuilds the reach limits and clears `stale`: for every particle of the cloth that is not held, `reach_to` is the held particle of the same cloth whose `rest` position is nearest to the particle's `rest` position and `reach` is that distance; -1 when the cloth has no held particle. Held particles get -1.

`contact(i int, cx real, cy real, cz real, r real, friction real)` keeps particle i (three numbers at `pos[i * 3]`) at least r from the point (cx, cy, cz): when its distance d is below r, and above 1e-12, it moves straight away from the point to distance r. Then friction: take the particle's movement this substep (its position minus `old`), remove from it the part along the direction from the point to the particle, and what remains is the slide; when the slide is longer than 1e-12, move the particle back along the slide by the smaller of the slide's length and `friction * (r - d)`.

`step(c int, dt real, wx real, wy real, wz real)` advances cloth c by dt seconds in a wind of velocity (wx, wy, wz). Nothing happens when dt is not above 0 or the cloth has no particles.
- First: when the cloth is stale and `limit` is true, run `limits`. Copy the cloth's `pos` to `from`, and also to `last` unless `keep` is true.
- Wind, once for the whole step: zero the cloth's `push`. For each triangle: n is its cross product as in `normals`, its area A is half of n's length (skip it when the length is below 1e-20), and n is scaled to length one; u is the wind minus the mean velocity of its three corners; s is the dot product of u and n; the force's size is the smaller of `0.5 * air * drag * A * s * s` and `density * A * abs(s) / dt`, and it points along n when s is positive and against n when s is negative. Add a third of the force times the particle's `inv` to the `push` of each of its three particles.
- Then `substeps` times (at least once), with h being dt over that count and k the substep's number counting from 1:
  - Every particle: copy `pos` to `old`. A held particle moves to the point a fraction k over the count of the way from `from` to `goal` (exactly `goal` in the last substep) and its velocity is zero. A free particle adds `h * push` and, on y, minus `h * gravity` to its velocity, multiplies the velocity by `max(1 - damping * h, 0)`, and adds h times the velocity to its position.
  - Every edge of the cloth, in stored order: with a and b its particles, d the vector from a to b, l its length (skip when below 1e-12) and W the sum of the two `inv` values (skip when 0): `m = (l - edge_len) / (l * (W + stretch / (h * h)))`; a moves by `d * (m * inv[a])` and b by `d * (-m * inv[b])`.
  - Every bend, the same with `bend_len` and `bend` in place of `edge_len` and `stretch`.
  - When `limit` is true, every particle with `reach_to` at least 0: when it is farther from that particle's position than `reach` (and the distance is above 1e-12), it moves straight toward it until the distance equals `reach`.
  - Colliders, for every free particle: each sphere through `contact` with its center and its radius plus `thickness`; each capsule through `contact` with the point of its segment nearest to the particle and its radius plus `thickness`; and with the floor on, a particle below `floor_y + thickness` is lifted to that height by a depth e, after which its movement this substep along x and z, when longer than 1e-12, is shortened by the smaller of its length and `friction * e`.
  - Every free particle's velocity becomes its position minus `old`, over h.
- Last: run `normals`.

`write(c int, p, stride int, at int, at_n int, out)` (p: read-only f32 slice, the mesh's vertices as given to `make`, with vertex v's normal at `p[v * stride + at_n]`; out: writable f32 slice of at least `nv * 16` floats) writes the cloth as two morph targets of eight floats per vertex. For vertex v with particle i, target one at `out[v * 8]`: `pos` of i minus the vertex's position, then 0, then `nrm` of i minus the vertex's normal, then 0. Target two at `out[(nv + v) * 8]`: `last` of i minus the vertex's position, then five zeros.

Readers: `count(c int) int` is the cloth's particle count; `particle(c int, vertex int) int` is the mesh vertex's particle as an index into the shared arrays; `stretch_now(c int) real` is the largest, over the cloth's edges, of the edge's current length over its stored length (1.0 with no edges); `speed(c int) real` is the mean length of the cloth's particle velocities (0 with no particles); `bounds(c int)` returns six reals, the lowest x, y, z and the highest x, y, z of the cloth's particle positions (zeros with no particles).

`fn main()` is not part of this library; do not write one.
